                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Cheyenne WY",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 18 at 9:12PM MDT until September 18 at 11:15PM MDT by NWS Cheyenne WY",
                "description": "FFWCYS\n\nThe National Weather Service in Cheyenne has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe South Fork Burn Scar in...\nWestern Dawes County in the panhandle of Nebraska...\nEast Central Sioux County in the panhandle of Nebraska...\n\n* Until 1115 PM MDT.\n\n* At 912 PM MDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain over the South Fork Burn Scar. Between 0.5 and 1 inch of\nrain has fallen. Additional rainfall amounts up to 1.5 inches are\npossible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected\nto begin shortly. Excessive rainfall over the burn scar will result\nin debris flow moving through the Soldier Creek and the White River\ndrainages. The debris flow can consist of rock, mud, vegetation and\nother loose materials.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms in and around the\nSouth Fork Burn Scar.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Flooding of areas in and around the South Fork Burn Scar.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nCrawford, Fort Robinson State Park and Soldier Creek Campground.",
                "instruction": "This is a life threatening situation. Heavy rainfall will cause\nextensive and severe flash flooding of creeks...streams...and ditches\nin the South Fork Burn Scar. Severe debris flows can also be\nanticipated across roads. Roads and driveways may be washed away in\nplaces. If you encounter flood waters...climb to safety.\n\nFlooding is occurring or is imminent. It is important to know where\nyou are relative to streams, rivers, or creeks which can become\nkillers in heavy rains. Campers and hikers should avoid streams or\ncreeks.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
